              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Calcium is an essential mineral that your body needs to build and maintain strong bones and teeth, regulate your heart rhythm, and help muscles and nerves function properly. \n\nFor most adults, the **Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) is 1,000 mg of calcium per day**, which increases to **1,200 mg per day** for women over 50 and men over 70.\n\nA wide variety of foods\u2014both dairy and non-dairy\u2014can help you meet your daily calcium needs. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Dairy Products\nDairy is one of the most readily absorbed (bioavailable) and concentrated sources of calcium.\n* **Yogurt (plain, low-fat):** ~450 mg per cup (8 oz)\n* **Ricotta Cheese:** ~335 mg per 1/2 cup\n* **Milk (skim, low-fat, or whole):** ~300 mg per cup (8 oz)\n* **Hard Cheeses (Cheddar, Swiss, Monterey Jack):** ~200 to 270 mg per ounce\n* **Mozzarella:** ~200 to 210 mg per ounce\n\n### 2. Seafood (with bones)\nCanned fish that contains soft, edible bones is an exceptional, dairy-free source of calcium.\n* **Canned Sardines (with bones):** ~325 to 370 mg per 3 ounces\n* **Canned Salmon (with bones):** ~180 to 210 mg per 3 ounces\n\n### 3. Plant-Based Greens & Vegetables\nMany leafy greens and vegetables are rich in calcium. *Note: See the absorption tip below regarding oxalates.*\n* **Collard Greens (cooked):** ~268 mg per cup\n* **Bok Choy / Pak Choi (cooked):** ~160 to 185 mg per cup\n* **Kale (cooked):** ~177 to 180 mg per cup\n* **Broccoli (cooked):** ~60 to 100 mg per cup\n\n### 4. Beans, Nuts, and Seeds\n* **Tofu (prepared with calcium sulfate/calcium-set):** ~250 to 750 mg per 4 ounces (check the label, as calcium content varies wildly based on how it is prepared)\n* **Chia Seeds:** ~180 mg per ounce (about 2 tablespoons)\n* **White Beans (cooked):** ~130 mg per cup\n* **Almonds:** ~75 to 80 mg per ounce (about 23 almonds)\n\n### 5. Fortified Foods and Drinks\nFor vegans, vegetarians, or those with lactose intolerance, fortified foods are highly effective alternatives.\n* **Fortified Plant Milks (Soy, Almond, Oat):** ~300 to 450 mg per cup (8 oz)\n* **Fortified Orange Juice:** ~300 mg per cup (8 oz)\n* **Fortified Oatmeal:** ~100 to 150 mg per packet\n* **Fortified Breakfast Cereals:** ~100 to 1,000 mg per serving (depending heavily on the brand)\n\n---\n\n### Tips for Maximizing Calcium Absorption\n\n* **Pair it with Vitamin D:** Your body cannot absorb calcium properly without Vitamin D. You can get Vitamin D from sensible sun exposure, egg yolks, fatty fish, or fortified foods (like milk and orange juice).\n* **Watch out for \"Oxalates\":** Some healthy foods like **spinach**, beet greens, and rhubarb contain plenty of calcium, but they also contain high amounts of oxalic acid (oxalates). Oxalates bind to calcium, making it difficult for your body to absorb. Opt for low-oxalate greens like **kale, bok choy, and collard greens** for better absorption.\n* **Limit portion sizes:** Your body absorbs calcium most efficiently in doses of **500 mg or less at one time**. If you are trying to increase your intake, it is better to spread your calcium-rich foods throughout the day rather than eating them all in one meal.",
